{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_referencing-values-with-refs","statement":"You add a ref to a component by importing the useRef Hook from React.","source_span":"import { useRef } from 'react';"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_referencing-values-with-refs","statement":"useRef takes the initial value you want to reference as its only argument, e.g. useRef(0).","source_span":"const ref = useRef(0);"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_referencing-values-with-refs","statement":"useRef returns an object like { current: 0 } holding the value passed to it.","source_span":"current: 0 // The value you passed to useRef"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_referencing-values-with-refs","statement":"A ref's current value is accessed via ref.current and is intentionally mutable, meaning you can both read and write to it.","source_span":"You can access the current value of that ref through the `ref.current` property. This value is intentionally mutable, meaning you can both read and write to it."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_referencing-values-with-refs","statement":"Changing a ref does not cause the component to re-render, unlike setting state.","source_span":"Changing a ref does not!"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_referencing-values-with-refs","statement":"In the stopwatch example, startTime and now are kept in state because that information is used for rendering.","source_span":"This information is used for rendering, so you'll keep it in state"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_referencing-values-with-refs","statement":"The stopwatch updates the current time every 10 milliseconds using setInterval, calling setNow(Date.now()).","source_span":"// Update the current time every 10ms.\n      setNow(Date.now());\n    }, 10);"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_referencing-values-with-refs","statement":"The interval ID returned by setInterval is kept in a ref (intervalRef) because it is not used for rendering.","source_span":"Since the interval ID is not used for rendering, you can keep it in a ref"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_referencing-values-with-refs","statement":"useState(initialValue) returns the current value of a state variable and a state setter function, unlike useRef which returns only an object with a current property.","source_span":"`useState(initialValue)` returns the current value of a state variable and a state setter function ( `[value, setValue]`)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_referencing-values-with-refs","statement":"You shouldn't read or write a ref's current value during rendering, whereas you can read state at any time.","source_span":"You shouldn't read (or write) the `current` value during rendering."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_referencing-values-with-refs","fact_refs":[0,1,2],"question":"How do you add a ref to a React component and what does useRef return?","answer":"Import useRef from React and call it with an initial value, e.g. const ref = useRef(0). It returns an object like { current: 0 } holding the value you passed in."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_referencing-values-with-refs","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Does changing a ref's value cause a React component to re-render?","answer":"No, changing a ref does not cause the component to re-render, unlike setting state which does."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_referencing-values-with-refs","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Why does the stopwatch example store startTime and now in state instead of a ref?","answer":"Because that information is used for rendering (to display the elapsed time), so it needs to be kept in state."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_referencing-values-with-refs","fact_refs":[6],"question":"How often and how does the stopwatch example update the current time?","answer":"It updates every 10 milliseconds using setInterval, which calls setNow(Date.now())."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_referencing-values-with-refs","fact_refs":[7],"question":"Why is the interval ID stored in a ref rather than in state in the stopwatch example?","answer":"Because the interval ID is not used for rendering, so keeping it in a ref avoids triggering unnecessary re-renders."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_referencing-values-with-refs","fact_refs":[9],"question":"Can you read or write a ref's current value while a component is rendering?","answer":"No, you shouldn't read or write a ref's current value during rendering, whereas state can be read at any time."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_removing-effect-dependencies","statement":"The React linter verifies that every reactive value (like props and state) read by an Effect is included in that Effect's dependency list.","source_span":"the linter will verify that you've included every reactive value (like props and state) that the Effect reads in the list of your Effect's dependencies"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_removing-effect-dependencies","statement":"Unnecessary dependencies may cause an Effect to run too often, or even create an infinite loop.","source_span":"Unnecessary dependencies may cause your Effect to run too often, or even create an infinite loop."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_removing-effect-dependencies","statement":"In the ChatRoom example, serverUrl is set to 'https://localhost:1234'.","source_span":"const serverUrl = 'https://localhost:1234';"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_removing-effect-dependencies","statement":"If you leave an Effect's dependency array empty ([]), the linter will suggest the correct dependencies to fill in.","source_span":"if you leave the Effect dependencies empty (`[]`), the linter will suggest the correct dependencies"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_removing-effect-dependencies","statement":"Since roomId is a reactive value that can change due to a re-render, the linter requires it to be declared as an Effect dependency ([roomId]).","source_span":"Since `roomId` is a reactive value (it can change due to a re-render), the linter verifies that you've specified it as a dependency."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_removing-effect-dependencies","statement":"If roomId receives a different value, React will re-synchronize the Effect so the chat stays connected to the selected room.","source_span":"If `roomId` receives a different value, React will re-synchronize your Effect. This ensures that the chat stays connected to the selected room"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_removing-effect-dependencies","statement":"Reactive values include props and all variables and functions declared directly inside the component body.","source_span":"Reactive values include props and all variables and functions declared directly inside of your component."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_removing-effect-dependencies","statement":"Omitting roomId from the dependency array triggers the linter error \"React Hook useEffect has a missing dependency: 'roomId'\".","source_span":"// 🔴 React Hook useEffect has a missing dependency: 'roomId'"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_removing-effect-dependencies","statement":"To remove a dependency, you must prove to the linter it isn't needed, for example by moving roomId out of the component so it's no longer reactive.","source_span":"you can move `roomId` out of your component to prove that it's not reactive and won't change on re-renders"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_removing-effect-dependencies","statement":"The example ChatRoom dropdown offers three room options: general, travel, and music.","source_span":"<option value=\"general\">general</option>\n          <option value=\"travel\">travel</option>\n          <option value=\"music\">music</option>"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_removing-effect-dependencies","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What does the React Effect dependency linter check for?","answer":"It verifies that every reactive value (such as props and state) read inside the Effect is included in that Effect's dependency list."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_removing-effect-dependencies","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What can go wrong if an Effect has unnecessary dependencies?","answer":"Unnecessary dependencies may cause the Effect to run too often, or even create an infinite loop."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_removing-effect-dependencies","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What happens if you leave a React Effect's dependency array empty?","answer":"The linter will suggest the correct dependencies to add based on the reactive values the Effect actually reads."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_removing-effect-dependencies","fact_refs":[4,5],"question":"Why must roomId be included in the ChatRoom Effect's dependency array, and what happens when it changes?","answer":"roomId is a reactive prop that can change on re-render, so the linter requires it as a dependency; when roomId receives a different value, React re-synchronizes the Effect so the chat stays connected to the selected room."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_removing-effect-dependencies","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What counts as a \"reactive value\" in a React component according to this guide?","answer":"Reactive values include props and all variables and functions declared directly inside the component body."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_removing-effect-dependencies","fact_refs":[8],"question":"How can you remove roomId from an Effect's dependency list without triggering a linter error?","answer":"By moving roomId out of the component (e.g., as a module-level constant) so it's no longer a reactive value and won't change on re-renders."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_render-and-commit","statement":"Displaying UI in React involves three steps: triggering a render, rendering the component, and committing changes to the DOM.","source_span":"1. **Triggering** a render (delivering the guest's order to the kitchen)\n2. **Rendering** the component (preparing the order in the kitchen)\n3. **Committing** to the DOM (placing the order on the table)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_render-and-commit","statement":"There are two reasons a component renders: it's the component's initial render, or the component's (or an ancestor's) state has been updated.","source_span":"It's the component's **initial render.**... The component's (or one of its ancestors') **state has been updated.**"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_render-and-commit","statement":"The initial render is triggered by calling createRoot with the target DOM node and then calling its render method with the component.","source_span":"it's done by calling [`createRoot`](/reference/react-dom/client/createRoot) with the target DOM node, and then calling its `render` method with your component"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_render-and-commit","statement":"Updating a component's state with the set function automatically queues a render.","source_span":"Updating your component's state automatically queues a render."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_render-and-commit","statement":"\"Rendering\" means React calling your components.","source_span":"**\"Rendering\" is React calling your components.**"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_render-and-commit","statement":"On the initial render React calls the root component; for subsequent renders React calls the function component whose state update triggered the render.","source_span":"On initial render, React will call the root component.\n* **For subsequent renders,** React will call the function component whose state update triggered the render."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_render-and-commit","statement":"Rendering must always be a pure calculation: given the same inputs a component must return the same JSX, and it must not change objects or variables that existed before rendering.","source_span":"Rendering must always be a [pure calculation]... * **Same inputs, same output.**... * **It minds its own business.**"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_render-and-commit","statement":"In Strict Mode, React calls each component's function twice to help surface mistakes caused by impure functions.","source_span":"When developing in \"Strict Mode\", React calls each component's function twice, which can help surface mistakes caused by impure functions."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_render-and-commit","statement":"During the initial render in the example, React creates DOM nodes for a <section>, an <h1>, and three <img> tags.","source_span":"During the initial render, React will [create the DOM nodes](https://developer.mozilla.org/docs/Web/API/Document/createElement) for `<section>`, `<h1>`, and three `<img>` tags."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_render-and-commit","statement":"During a re-render, React calculates which properties have changed since the previous render but doesn't act on that information until the commit phase.","source_span":"During a re-render, React will calculate which of their properties, if any, have changed since the previous render. It won't do anything with that information until the next step, the commit phase."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_render-and-commit","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What are the three steps React takes to display UI on screen?","answer":"Triggering a render, rendering the component, and committing changes to the DOM."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_render-and-commit","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What are the two reasons a React component renders?","answer":"Either it's the component's initial render, or the component's (or one of its ancestors') state has been updated."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_render-and-commit","fact_refs":[2],"question":"How is the initial render of a React app triggered?","answer":"By calling createRoot with the target DOM node and then calling its render method with the component."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_render-and-commit","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Which component does React call on the initial render versus a subsequent re-render?","answer":"On the initial render React calls the root component; on subsequent renders it calls the function component whose state update triggered the render."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_render-and-commit","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What two properties must rendering satisfy to be considered a pure calculation in React?","answer":"Given the same inputs, a component must always return the same JSX (same inputs, same output), and it must not change any objects or variables that existed before rendering."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_render-and-commit","fact_refs":[6],"question":"Why does React call each component's function twice in Strict Mode?","answer":"To help surface mistakes caused by impure functions."}
